{"name":"Cherub","hex":"#f5d7dc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cherub","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A winged creature attending God and guarding his throne described as a being with four faces (man, lion, ox, and eagle), human hands, calf hooves, four wings, and many eyes. A description can be found in Ezekiel chapter 1 and Ezekiel chapter 10; similar to a lamassu (winged bull with a human torso) in the pre-exilic texts of the Hebrew Bible, more humanoid in later texts.","A winged angel, described by Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite (c. 5th–6th century) as the second highest order of angels, ranked above thrones and below seraphim."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cherub"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cherub","blocks":[{"text":"A cherub is one type of supernatural being in the Abrahamic religions. The numerous depictions of cherubim assign to them many different roles, such as protecting the entrance of the Garden of Eden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tetramorph_meteora.jpg/330px-Tetramorph_meteora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherub"},{"title":"CHERUB","blocks":[{"text":"CHERUB is a series of teenage spy novels written by English author Robert Muchamore, focusing around a fictional division of the British Security Service called CHERUB, which employs children, predominantly orphans, as intelligence agents."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3f/CHERUB_Logo.svg/330px-CHERUB_Logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CHERUB"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Ivory_plaque_from_Megiddo_IAA_1938-780.jpg/500px-Ivory_plaque_from_Megiddo_IAA_1938-780.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.